Hi Linda:

Regarding your long-term design.  Remember the two aspects to the=20
estimation of abundance for your marsupials: detection function and=20
replicate transects.  I appreciate that with endangered species it is=20
challenging to accumulate sufficient detections to do a good job of=20
fitting a detection function (that was the purpose of walking transects=20

multiple times as you note).

On the face of it, you may not need to repeatedly walk transects, as you

propose in your new design. My concern with your proposal however, is=20
that you ensure you have sufficient detections within a survey window=20
(which you describe as occurring every year or two), so that you do not=20
need to pool detections across more than one or two years to fit a=20
detection function.  As you can guess, if you need to accumulate=20
sightings over 3 or 4 years to fit your detection function, you need to=20
assume that the shape of that detection function does not change over=20
that period of time.  The longer the time needed to accumulate=20
detections for fitting, the more tenuous the homogeneity of that=20
function becomes.

I trust you are also going to try to get even spatial coverage in your=20
surveys henceforth as well.  So I'm afraid you need to incorporate both=20
adequate spatial coverage *and* sufficient detections for confident=20

detection function fitting into your future survey protocol.

Hope you find the advice useful.

